    "cue": "A clinician is reviewing the pathophysiology and mechanisms of Diarrhea and metabolic acidosis.",
    "task": "Which explanation best describes the dominant pathophysiology of this condition?",
    "answer": "Loss of bicarbonate-rich intestinal fluid can produce normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis.",
    "foils": [
      "Vomiting removes gastric hydrochloric acid, increasing the relative bicarbonate concentration and producing metabolic alkalosis.",
      "Impaired digestion or absorption of lipids leaves excess fat within the stool.",
      "Slow colonic transit or impaired evacuation increases stool water absorption and hardness.",
      "Failure of lower esophageal sphincter relaxation plus impaired peristalsis causes esophageal retention.",
      "A portion of stomach moves through the diaphragm into the thoracic cavity.",
      "Transient LES relaxation or weak sphincter tone permits gastric contents to contact distal esophageal mucosa.",
      "Acid-peptic damage breaches the protective duodenal mucosal lining.",
      "Patchy skip lesions may involve any GI segment and extend through the entire intestinal wall.",
      "Continuous mucosal inflammation begins at the rectum and extends proximally through the colon.",
      "Inflammation of diverticular outpouchings may produce localized infection.",
      "Obstructed outflow increases pressure, reduces perfusion, and can cause ischemia, bacterial invasion, and rupture.",
      "Fibrotic resistance diverts portal blood into collateral veins, producing enlarged gastroesophageal varices.",
      "Portosystemic shunting and impaired hepatic clearance allow neurotoxins such as ammonia to affect brain function.",
      "Elevated portal hydrostatic pressure, splanchnic vasodilation, renal sodium retention, and low albumin contribute to peritoneal fluid buildup.",
      "Digestion of upper GI blood can produce melena; rapid bleeding may cause hematemesis."
    ],
