    "task": "Which explanation best describes the dominant pathophysiology of this condition?",
    "answer": "A sprain is ligament damage; a strain involves muscle or tendon rather than a ligament.",
    "foils": [
      "The articular surfaces lose part, but not all, of their normal contact.",
      "Microscopic to complete muscle/tendon disruption triggers local inflammatory pain.",
      "The ends of bones completely lose normal joint contact.",
      "Healing progresses from hematoma and inflammation to reparative callus and remodeling.",
      "Osteoblasts lay down osteoid; osteoclasts resorb mineralized bone.",
      "A disrupted skin barrier exposes fractured bone and tissues to environmental contamination.",
      "Raised pressure within fascia impairs microvascular tissue perfusion and causes ischemia.",
      "Microvascular perfusion can fail well before distal pulses disappear.",
      "Injured myocytes release myoglobin, creatine kinase, and potassium into circulation.",
      "Bone resorption exceeds formation, lowering bone mineral density and strength.",
      "PTH increases serum calcium through bone resorption, renal handling, and vitamin D activation.",
      "Failure to mineralize new osteoid leaves adult bone abnormally soft despite ongoing remodeling.",
      "Bone infection causes inflammation and necrosis; a sequestrum is a separated fragment of dead bone.",
      "Chronic inflammatory synovial pannus damages cartilage and bone.",
      "Progressive cartilage and whole-joint degeneration produce mechanical joint symptoms."
    ],
